KeyCaps macro for KMAC

Introduction
KeyCaps is an OPL based macro for the KMAC (Keyboard MACro) app.
It is designed for all of those people (like me) who use indirect characters
(e.g. † ¨ © « ² Ò ñ õ etc.) a lot, either for non-English documents,
programming or any other reason. KeyCaps is an easy to use alternative
to the difficult to remember "Control-nnn" and "Control-nx" combinations.

Installation
1) Add the following lines to your main KMAC macro file:

MACRO KeyCaps
KEY C (Or whatever key should be used)
QUIET (Not essential, but tidy)
CONTENTS
oM:\OPO\KEYCAPS.OPO  (See Note below)

Or in KMAC's shorhand form:

M KeyCaps
K C
Q
C
oM:\OPO\KEYCAPS.OPO

2) Place the "KEYCAPS.OPO" file in the OPO directory on the internal drive.

Note: the OPO file can be placed anywhere provided the last line of
      the macro is changed accordingly.

Using KeyCaps
Once KeyCaps is invoked by use of the appropriate hot-key, you will be
prompted for the font type to use, Swiss or Roman. The only differerence
is their appearance.

The screen is divided into two areas, the buffer the top, the selection
area at the bottom.

In KeyCaps most of the keys that can normally be entered directly (e.g. A-Z,
0-9 etc.) can be typed into the buffer as in normal typing. These keys
comprise the first three rows, except for the delete character (last on
third line).

However several keys have special purposes:
The cursor keys move the character highlight (wrap around).
The cursor keys with shift move the character highlight 4 spaces (wrap around).
Enter will place the highlighted character into the buffer.
Delete will remove the last character in the buffer.
Esc will exit KeyCaps without pasting the buffer.
Tab will exit KeyCaps after pasting the buffer.
Help will describe the key functions.

Note: The buffer at present has a limit of 127 characters. This can be
circumvented by doing blocks of roughly 100 charcters, pasting them,
then re-activating KeyCaps (not elegant, but it works.)

